    },
    {
      "id": "ms7chivqjossvokl9i",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1094,
      "y": 1010,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "text": "≈ 2.48 V when released\n3.3 V × 3 / (1 + 3)",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "createdAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "version": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ms7chivqj6mid92242",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 1140,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "text": "Series resistor is 1 kΩ, NOT the arm circuit's 10 kΩ. The\nsource here is the Pi's own 3.3 V rail, so the divider must\nbe recomputed: 10 kΩ would give ≈ 0.76 V, below V_IH, so\nthe pin would read \"pressed\" permanently and the system\ncould never leave SAFE_HALT. If your bench was built from\nan older drawing, replace it.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "createdAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "version": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ms7chivq0owo7i7ogq2a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 470,
      "y": 1140,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "text": "Pressed, or a broken wire → the 3 kΩ pulls the\npin LOW → SAFE_HALT.\n\nFail-safe: a pressed button and a severed wire\nproduce the same safe state, which is the whole\nreason the pull-down is there.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "createdAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "version": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ms7chivq0bjcry9h8f8e",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 900,
      "y": 1140,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "text": "Pole 1 of the same mushroom breaks the K1 contactor\ncoil circuit (drawing 2). This pole is sense only — it\ntells the software, it does not remove power.\n\nA monitored-safety-relay architecture would need a\nthird contact, or this sense would move to a K1 aux.",
      "fontSize": 12,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "createdAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "version": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "ms7chivq732rw7hglvm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 1268,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "text": "Door interlock contact 2 sits between the arm switch and the sense tap, so an open door reads as a disarm on GPIO 24. The existing\nFiringController::set_armed(false) path handles it — no door GPIO, no code change. Contact 1 is in the K1 coil circuit (drawing 2).",
      "fontSize": 13,
      "fontFamily": 2,
      "createdAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T10:02:31.773Z",
      "version": 1
    }
  ],
  "appState": {
    "viewBackgroundColor": "#ffffff",
    "gridSize": null
  }
}